#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069905} :activity/id #uuid "347053fb-b31c-5623-b4db-4c770ada4d18" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049069 :topic/id #uuid "d7927711-e084-5b0c-97d6-05acd9686522" :topic/math-academy-id 416} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070031 :tutorial/content "We know that we can use the method of integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Now, suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2e^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Can we use integration by parts in this case?\n\nLet's  try. We define $u$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for integration by parts leads to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe still don't have an answer, but the problem is reduced to the calculation of a simpler integral  $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ To compute this integral, we just need to apply the integration by parts formula again. For this second integral, we take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{8}\\,x{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}-\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}(8{x}^{2}-4x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{C_1}{2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "35a2eb7b-569b-5939-bcc0-2261596617e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 901 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "944ebf60-fe41-58ac-91a8-63c93b565add" :step/math-academy-id 655420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068636 :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547046}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"} :step/id 