a-978d-5956-a57b-742f51ee2e89" :step/math-academy-id 618992}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101060}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516931} :activity/id #uuid "42b474a5-8681-5ff6-964e-1f1f239512b0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280 :topic/id #uuid "e992dbdf-067e-5347-82bc-a58321ce8275" :topic/math-academy-id 1203} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e03faf3-3e92-514d-a666-b56caff650f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295437} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae20b4-51bc-53f3-a56b-409b71e224ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516550} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fed9e6-b6f9-583b-951d-71398609b0ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a9a63b25-eb4e-543f-9169-7fae6ed27cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-316302" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)={cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{{u}^{9}}{9})|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }[(-1)^{9}-{1}^{9}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a91efd6-aedd-507e-aa7c-d5175154e395" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339eefdd-be81-5592-a01a-49ac8b31465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295558} :answer-field/id #uuid "7617b7a0-eac2-59c6-b90b-60cddec4dd19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0bab2f3-434f-5bc7-bbe6-28166bd448b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-316303" :question/problem "Find the value of $c$ such that the average value of $h(x)=10x-4$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $6.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{c}(10x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c}(5{x}^{2}-4x){|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(5{c}^{2}-4c)-0] \\\\ 6 & =5c-4 \\\\ 10 & =5c \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20a5bad1-0380-5cbe-880b-37b9d31deb30" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e8b462-2441-51eb-ae60-4aff0a2f6fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315531} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d94eeb7-653c-5604-8ed4-f4eb869367bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0199315-7b83-568e-8a38-7bc403eadc2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344715" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfa665cf99f35e5cd2edef04b3f334f0054bbce7fc9b66c519d59d30e77ba2a1.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[0,10],$ is shown above. It consists of line segments.\n\nFind the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of $f(x)$ on $[1,9].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [5,9].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-4+6 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ea34d17-8a70-5fd7-9907-80cd1fee528f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d4cfcd-4ddc-537e-b949-5338d53ece3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516213} {:answer/id #uuid "32a1e531-1ccd-5a93-b077-640516633b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516218} {:answer/id #uuid "55d8a23c-b830-57d0-a5c7-64f48c868528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516293} {:answer/id #uuid "ada54ccc-dafd-57ee-a1f0-3847e2e0224c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516473} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd390d4-2d1d-5cf8-8e2d-09168ddad98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516218} :answer-field/id #uuid "32499d7d-98ec-5c26-b24c-0196cd9865ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab409300-c391-5504-81d8-684da401e891" :question/math-academy-id "q-120716" :question/problem "Find the numbers $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=24{x}^{2}+20x+4$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $7.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 7 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(24{x}^{2}+20x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 7 & ={\\frac{1}{c}(8{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}+4x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 7 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(8{c}^{3}+10{c}^{2}+4c)-0] \\\\ 7 & =8{c}^{2}+10c+4 \\\\ 0 & =8{c}^{2}+10c-3 \\\\ 0 & =(2c+3)(4c-1) \\\\ c & =-\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $c>0,$ we conclude that $c=\\frac{1}{4}$ only."} :step/id #uuid "839c2a35-6ec1-5d74-a95d-02cfbee1b3eb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516877}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2abe4d-57c0-599d-b2da-3bc3a8e57a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316437} {:answer/id #uuid "9019925a-fde9-5e42-9741-5b09732117ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516414} :answer-field/id #uuid "492b509c-6314-53ae-a2da-64e59ff1f2cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce1dc838-ac3c-58a3-9be3-5a30e3211516" :question/math-academy-id "q-344695" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{\\pi }(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d90c070-8a52-56ce-b438-2598dfd524f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516895}}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516095}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176802} :activity/id #uuid "ae699ea5-a992-5568-9ccc-711c674348a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050011 :topic/id #uuid "2609ace5-5513-5f84-a7cf-f0413860fe14" :topic/math-academy-id 3551} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175803 :question/id #uuid "8c4777a6-d831-54cc-8677-b05ea45b2b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7324"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516842}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"} :step/id #uuid "d19c0f9f-80cc-5cf8-9f9a-1685e2ae53c0" :step/math-academy-id 27108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175875 :question/id #uuid "6f96059e-ce3d-5771-b3d8-15044cfd5495" :question/math-academy-id "e-9375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541098}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"} :step/id #uuid "dd1fcac6-2708-5449-8133-f0e4daba018d" :step/math-academy-id 35102 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175876 :question/id #uuid "2096869c-2a41-51a5-b2e8-c1039af4d640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9376"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480697}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"} :step/id #uuid "0b510a99-00f3-5621-8ddb-4da0432e0388" :step/math-academy-id 35103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177031 :tutorial/content "In general, the logistic growth boundary value problem for the function $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y), \\qquad y(0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0,$ and $0 < y < a.$\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00478583a9bc76980b898db4f846f323511026c1c6ac7b79e3f047575a015305.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- In practical situations, we usually set $t > 0.$ If we relax this constraint, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to-\\infty}{y(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\n \nThis behavior can be confirmed using an analysis similar to what we saw earlier.\n\n- The parameter $a$ is the **carrying capacity,** and is the limiting value of $y(t)$ for large $t.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = a.\n$$\n\n \nIf $y(t)$ represents how some population changes with time, the carrying capacity gives the maximum possible population.\n\n- We have assumed that $0 < y < a$ for all $t.$ Other cases are possible, though we do not consider those here." :tutorial/id #uuid "3f070df6-1d9d-5050-b509-d48d330c4709" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5714 :tutorial/title "Some Key Features of a Logistic Growth Curve"} :step/id #uuid "2b3e0981-7dcf-5a80-b4a4-1a180100a2d1" :step/math-academy-id 35104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177033 :tutorial/content "For some differential equations, it's possible to sketch the solution curves *without* calculating the general solution. Instead, we gather information about the solutions using the differential equation itself.\n\nAs an example, consider the following logistic growth equation: \n\n$$\ny' = 5y(10-y)\n$$\n\nIn practical applications, we're usually interested in the domain $t > 0,$ so we will stick to this constraint for the moment.\n\nThe equation is autonomous, so let's start by finding the equilibrium solutions. Setting up the equation $y'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n5y(10-y) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, there are two equilibrium solutions, $y=0,$ and $y=10.$ Let's create a diagram and plot these two solutions.\n\n![](images/98/98d52420673e65eedf5679e57b9c562f8a8eeabde24d0d2ed4fff827a6447fc2.png)\n\nWe will now sketch a solution curve that lies between these two equilibrium solutions. \n\nNotice the following:\n\n- Since $0 < y < 10$ for all $t,$ we have that $5y(10-y)$ is positive for all $t.$\n\n- Therefore, $y'(t)$ is positive for all $t.$\n\n- Hence, $y(t)$ is increasing for all $t.$\n\nUsing this information, we can start to sketch a solution curve.\n\n![](images/ce/ce6f493f4c2830acf42e189ace04027f8452ec3df92d24b9d223f92848832996.png)\n\nNext, we note the following regarding the end-behavior of our solution:\n\n- The expression $5y(10-y)\\to 0$ as $y\\to10.$\n\n- Therefore, $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to 10.$\n\nIn other words, the curve flattens as $y$ approaches $10.$ Adding this behavior to our diagram, we get a complete sketch of the solution curve.\n\n![](images/f3/f329f3cd49d61c3a1e2838397521cdd6d3bbefc7dadd09d1dd25b00767a0cd37.png)\n\nGathering information about the solutions to a differential equation without solving it is called **qualitative analysis.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "05ebc248-7295-5381-964a-30c7e3b638e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ad747121-27b0-5c58-8372-2eae12c1e5f1" :step/math-academy-id 35114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177034 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to the following logistic growth equation:\n\n$$\ny' = 5y(10-y), \\qquad y(0) = y_0.\n$$\n\nThe logistic growth curve for this problem is sketched below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a225fd04f81bd65e1bad3ff14097563d112a16e3847aac26cc86591533ea5d0.png)\n\nThe curve contains an inflection point, that is, a point where $y(t)$ is growing at its fastest possible rate. What information can we deduce about this point? \n\nTo find the inflection point, we solve $y''(t) = 0.$ First, we differentiate the differential equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =(5y)^{′}\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (10-y)^{′} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (10-y)\\cdot {y}^{′}+(5y)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {y}^{′} \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (5\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (50-5y-5y) \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (50-10y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to solve $y''(t) = 0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny'\\cdot\\left(50-10y\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe know that $y'$ is increasing for all $t,$ so $y'\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n50-10y = 0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad y = 5.\n$$\n\nLet's mark this on our diagram.\n\n![](images/75/75d0363483e1bda70decb171466fe9e506eb0da80d0e78ed893bb61551ae9d44.png)\n\nThe fact that the inflection point occurs when $y$ is precisely half the carrying capacity is *not* a coincidence. In fact, this is always the case. The general situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7754f42adbb938c1d07ab2636a4c24f1cd0bc8536c974af2497e997589efa115.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9032224d-2537-52e4-a688-8e341433ca18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5719 :tutorial/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase"} :step/id #uuid "ce23de13-efb4-50d3-a7f4-3e6500a63fbd" :step/math-academy-id 35116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176684}}] :activity/title "Qualitative Analysis of the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176102}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516918} :activity/id #uuid "df4cdd91-8906-5e4a-b0e5-b2f695c09521" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050011 :topic/id #uuid "2609ace5-5513-5f84-a7cf-f0413860fe14" :topic/math-academy-id 3551} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02150670-428b-56e2-9985-6194ff4b8ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516116} {:answer/id #uuid "0c56636a-f906-5735-8c37-7b2138e8f77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516136} {:answer/id #uuid "4562e56f-79a7-5953-82dc-cdefccb01ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516253} {:answer/id #uuid "bf315d70-83f5-5ea5-abd9-3ae3cfc47fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516517} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2bf34-d828-5bcd-970b-c54ad54643d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "860" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516253} :answer-field/id #uuid "701ad097-1360-59f4-8891-d19c7b1ca762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4a129bc-20d0-5e0c-8e4d-26304c226b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-118558" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of beavers in a river follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}),\\,y(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible beaver population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}) \\\\  & =0.2y(\\frac{800-y}{100}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2y}{100}(800-y) \\\\  & =0.002y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the number of beavers cannot exceed $800.$"} :step/id #uuid "547b9566-7a7e-5c10-8c3a-e62cae9ff92e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fd38fb-b73c-5e14-af38-22ca47425e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516222} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd45c43-fd6b-5dfa-8ec5-d8595124bde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516232} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed5ba35-1c17-5530-84e8-dc60fa32a602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516409} {:answer/id #uuid "d449452a-bba6-5329-88f3-21fa31072558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "130\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516558} {:answer/id #uuid "f8137660-b678-5be7-9f4e-4e308f57cd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516558} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a62696-1e6e-54dd-bb76-a23cc2767801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "94d0a8c6-e1cc-589d-baf5-ae5d11bc5ecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-119848" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of elephants on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=26y-0.0002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=120,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of elephants?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =26y-0.0002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0002y(130\\,000-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=130\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $130\\,000,$ and we conclude that the number of elephants cannot exceed $130\\,000.$"} :step/id #uuid "6b2c3692-cb7d-5953-88f2-5e6b531cfc44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfd9676-eb74-5ebe-b031-0a788f78718e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516140} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4fb25d-27d2-5e5c-bcfd-c01b03fe7b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516233} {:answer/id #uuid "4b70a52a-3757-5bac-863c-6e7a9ca75c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516266} {:answer/id #uuid "8f83a6a6-3e56-5e96-b0ee-342a84b5b56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516413} {:answer/id #uuid "f4473ebd-4861-56d2-8435-0aad1abb7b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516266} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19ec431-2d31-55ac-9370-03580e2d85ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b63e7390-032d-5732-93f4-683f3a627416" :question/math-academy-id "q-15495" :question/problem "A rumor is spreading in a city. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ who heard the rumor follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=12P-0.000\\,2{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=2\\,000.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in hours. For which value of $P$ is the number of people who heard the rumor growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =12P-0.000\\,2{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,02P(60\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=60\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $60\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 60\\,000=30\\,000.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f1e6e85-1cc9-56f5-81c5-125a6a2d7028" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e11bfc06-4f07-5209-8c0b-4dd495bb95cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174090} {:answer/id #uuid "efd3be5e-7270-5d26-8763-b867cdbeae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174091} {:answer/id #uuid "2335c4d7-b3f8-5bed-b59f-86c5b955e524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174093} {:answer/id #uuid "67918e66-eb46-5022-8e71-699c7b27de4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516337} {:answer/id #uuid "c00bef54-cb61-5193-b9dc-726065db63fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186174090} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d72255e-ec91-5b72-bb1f-7ee2e464e74e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb420906-a342-521a-b8a1-6dfcb9eb4dbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-118474" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,000y-10{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=10y(100-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=100.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $100.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=100.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9fd0633c-ca54-5dbc-adf5-6d3546b046c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4083cc71-3bcb-5360-9978-e4ce2aebb68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516239} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8b8268-0d26-5cf4-8d60-0789f0a8afa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8a8e489-11e9-52ae-86cf-436bdb177f60" :question/math-academy-id "q-353486" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of elk in a protected national park follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5P-0.001{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the elk population growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =5P-0.001{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001P(5000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=5000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $5000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5000=2500.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4938297-4932-5247-bb96-9911519542a2"}] :activity/title "Qualitative Analysis of the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516109}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084269} :activity/id #uuid "b5963314-027e-58a8-94c3-2b38175169a4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198 :topic/id #uuid "35c03b2e-e860-5cb2-96d6-2e103a19283f" :topic/math-academy-id 788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084482 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is normally distributed. Then the probability density function $f(x)$ of $X$ is a unimodal, bell-shaped curve. Moreover, it is symmetrical about the mean, median, and mode, which all equal $\\mu$.  \n\n![](images/0c/0c8951446f52cea9db6c0a2b600fc53cb564c3d64ff0afab15c9756dc0c0f29b.png)\n\nWe can use the normal distribution to model a wide range of real-life situations because many real-world distributions are roughly bell-shaped and symmetrical around a mean value. \n\nPatients' blood pressures, people's weights, measurement errors, and IQ scores are just some of the real-world examples that can be modeled as a normal distribution. \n\nTo determine whether a given data is normal, the first thing to do is to check the shape of the distribution. For example, if a data set is roughly bell-shaped, then it *might* be normally distributed. \n\nIt should be noted that, in practice, statisticians will often need to perform several tests on the data to check for normality (these are called **normality tests**). We cannot rely only on a distribution's shape since other distributions are also bell-shaped. However, checking the shape is often a good starting point." :tutorial/id #uuid "9f150bce-c1b4-502c-bcb1-da067550b958" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4966 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "932c4862-d05e-5b90-85c8-d54c3a5130a8" :step/math-academy-id 30927 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083126 :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f-520f-8541-f328f9e63fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8279"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :kno